<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indonesia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Indonesian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Indonesia is an archipelago nation in South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> comprising thousands of islands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. Much of it was once under the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a>. It later became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, the centrepiece of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a>, and won independence after a revolution in the late 1940s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indonesia.jpg" alt="Indonesia" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jayakatwang · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Indonesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The memory of independence and of long resistance to the Dutch remained central to Acehnese identity, feeding the Darul Islam revolt of the 1950s and the Free Aceh Movement, whose conflict with <b>Indonesia</b> ended in the Helsinki peace agreement of 2005, after which Aceh gained special autonomy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch East Indies created the territorial frame of modern <b>Indonesia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Indonesia</b> the Dutch were present for some three and a half centuries, though effective control over much of the archipelago came only in the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Revolutionary Front for an Independent East Timor (Fretilin) declared independence on 28 November 1975; on 7 December the Indonesian military invaded, and in July 1976 <b>Indonesia</b> declared East Timor its 27th province.</blockquote><a href="/empire/east-timor">East Timor · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Indonesia</b> grew out of the Dutch East Indies, a colony assembled by the Netherlands over three centuries, and out of a nationalist movement that took shape in the early twentieth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its founders sought to lead the Muslim world, and though their 'Islamistan' found no takers, the OIC they wanted was formed in the 1970s; Pakistan backed independence movements from <b>Indonesia</b> to Algeria and championed the Palestinian cause.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
